Periorbital Cellulitis (pre and post-septal)
Opthalmology & ENT
Erythematous eyelid edema, Painful ophthalmoplegia/proptosis (post-septal), Intact vision/motility (pre-septal)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-033
Reason for Visit: Rapidly expanding, painful red swelling of the eyelid following a minor skin abrasion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed an acute, unilateral, tender, and intensely erythematous pronounced eyelid edema. This clinical picture demands immediate, face-to-face assessment to decisively differentiate pre-septal cellulitis from orbital (post-septal) cellulitis. The latter is an absolute vision- and life-threatening emergency requiring immediate IV antibiotics and surgical decompression to prevent optic nerve ischemia, cavernous sinus thrombosis, or intracranial extension.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My left eyelid swelled completely shut since this morning, it is bright red, and it throbs constantly."
History of Present Illness: Condition started 24 hours ago as a minor localized irritation following a suspected insect bite near the left eyebrow. Rapidly evolved into a unilateral, severe, tender, and erythematous pronounced eyelid edema. Patient denies high-grade fever, systemic severe malaise, or a toxic clinical appearance. Most importantly, the patient explicitly denies deep orbital pain, double vision, or pain explicitly exacerbated by voluntary eye movement.
Functional Status: Visually limited on the left due to mechanical obstruction by the swollen eyelid.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding potential vision loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable at rest, but guarding the left eye. Not appearing systemically toxic.
Physical Exam: Left upper and lower eyelids demonstrate pronounced, tense, erythematous, and warm edema. Upon manual elevation of the swollen eyelid, visual acuity is completely normal and symmetric (6/6). Pupillary light reflexes are brisk and reactive bilaterally; absolutely no relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD). Extraocular ocular motility is full, completely intact, and absolutely painless in all gazes, without any restriction or ophthalmoplegia. No proptosis (globe displacement). No chemosis (bulbar conjunctival swelling).
Environmental Observations: Patient applying ice packs to the eye.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pre-Septal Cellulitis - Status: Acute, localized infection
Differentials:

Orbital (Post-Septal) Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of proptosis, absolutely painless and full extraocular movements, intact visual acuity, normal pupillary reflexes, and lack of systemic toxicity or high fever.

Allergic Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of exquisite localized tenderness, marked unilateral warmth, and throbbing pain, whereas allergic reactions are typically intensely pruritic, non-tender, and often bilateral.

Acute External Hordeolum (Stye) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The diffuse involvement of both the upper and lower eyelids extending beyond the lid margin, lacking a single, focal, pointing pustule at an eyelash follicle.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook, rapidly progressive periorbital swelling, necessitating immediate evaluation to rule out post-septal extension. Direct clinical examination was critical to assess globe function and optic nerve integrity. The physical exam decisively confirmed normal visual acuity, brisk pupillary reflexes without an RAPD, and painless, full extraocular motility. These negative findings strictly localized the infection to the superficial, pre-septal tissues. By definitively ruling out orbital cellulitis, the urgent assessment prevented a traumatic, unnecessary emergency department admission and CT scan, allowing for immediate, safe initiation of high-dose oral antibiotics in the home setting.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Cephalexin 500mg QID for 7 days (with close clinical monitoring). Advised strict continuation of oral Paracetamol and Ibuprofen for pain and inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To accelerate localized immune response and drainage, the patient must apply a warm, moist compress to the eyelid for 15 minutes every 4 hours. The immediate soothing of the taut, inflamed skin provides rapid positive reinforcement, ensuring strict adherence to the mechanical therapy required to supplement the systemic antibiotics and resolve the localized superficial edema.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or Optometrist review in exactly 24 hours to ensure the infection is entirely responsive and not extending posteriorly.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops any pain whatsoever when looking up, down, or sideways, if double vision occurs, if the eye appears to bulge outward, or if visual acuity suddenly drops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes the infection remains in front of the orbital septum. Very close monitoring is required.
Clinical Course: The redness and swelling should cease expanding within 24 hours of starting antibiotics and begin noticeably shrinking by 48 hours.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The infection is currently trapped in the skin. If it pushes backward into the eye socket, it can cause blindness. If it starts hurting to move your eye, or if you see double, you must go to the emergency department immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Bacteria from a small scratch or bite have infected the soft skin of your eyelid. Because the skin here is so loose, it swells dramatically. The oral antibiotics will kill the bacteria before they can spread deeper into the eye socket.
Actionable Steps: Take the antibiotics strictly by the clock. Stop applying ice; switch to warm compresses to increase blood flow and deliver the antibiotics to the infected tissue. Do not attempt to squeeze or pop the swelling.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Pre-Septal Cellulitis (Eyelid skin infection). Start oral antibiotics immediately. See GP in 24 hours for review. If it hurts to move your eye, or vision blurs, go to ED.

SMS EDUCATION

Apply a warm, moist compress for 15 minutes, 4 times a day. Watch closely for danger signs: pain on eye movement, double vision, or a bulging eye. Take antibiotics strictly as prescribed.
